**Whole task:** fit a gripper and program a pick-and-place loop. Measure that it repeats.

## Parts

One gripper, chosen at the start of this milestone ([End effectors](https://docs.source-robotics.com/PAROL-docs/1.1/end-effectors/)):

- the [pneumatic gripper](https://docs.source-robotics.com/PAROL-docs/1.1/end-effectors/pneumatic-gripper/), which needs compressed air and a valve ([Pneumatics](https://docs.source-robotics.com/PAROL-docs/1.1/06-peripherals/pneumatics/));
- the [SSG-48](https://docs.source-robotics.com/PAROL-docs/1.1/end-effectors/ssg-48/) or MSG electric gripper, on the CAN bus at the forearm's M8 connector;
- a printed gripper of our own design with TPU fingers on the joint 6 coupler ([Custom tools](https://docs.source-robotics.com/PAROL-docs/1.1/end-effectors/custom-tools/)). This one is our design.

## Concepts

- **The tool frame and the TCP.** The software positions the tool centre point, so the tool must be registered with its offset from the flange ([Custom tools](https://docs.source-robotics.com/PAROL-docs/1.1/end-effectors/custom-tools/); [Reference frames](https://docs.source-robotics.com/PAROL-docs/1.1/theory/reference-frames/)).
- **Joint moves and Cartesian moves**, and what a repeatability of 0.1 mm means ([Specifications](https://docs.source-robotics.com/PAROL-docs/1.1/03-technical-data/specifications/); [Drives, ratios and resolution](https://docs.source-robotics.com/PAROL-docs/1.1/03-technical-data/drives-and-ratios/)).
- **The 10 ms packet cycle**, and why a slow PC makes the arm stutter ([What you need](https://docs.source-robotics.com/PAROL-docs/1.1/01-overview/what-you-need/)).

## How-to

- Gripper setup and the homing offset for the tool ([Homing, "Before you press Home"](https://docs.source-robotics.com/PAROL-docs/1.1/05-operation/homing/); [End effectors](https://docs.source-robotics.com/PAROL-docs/1.1/end-effectors/)).
- RBTscript programs in the commander ([Running programs](https://docs.source-robotics.com/PAROL-docs/1.1/05-operation/running-programs/); [RBTscript](https://docs.source-robotics.com/PAROL-docs/1.1/programming/03-commander/rbtscript/)).
- The [Python API](https://docs.source-robotics.com/PAROL-docs/1.1/programming/02-python-api/), and its [Tools and the TCP](https://docs.source-robotics.com/PAROL-docs/1.1/programming/02-python-api/tools-and-tcp/) page.

## Practice

- Teaching poses.
- Writing short scripts.
- The dial-indicator repeatability test.

## Done check

Twenty cycles of picking a 3.5 mm cable plug from a printed nest and placing it in a second nest. A dial gauge or feeler test shows the spread of the return-to-pose. Joel compares it to the spec and explains the gap.

**needs_source:** the measurement method. The official docs state the spec and give no test procedure.

## Terms

- **TCP** and **tool centre point**: the point on the tool that the software positions, set as a transform from the flange ([Custom tools](https://docs.source-robotics.com/PAROL-docs/1.1/end-effectors/custom-tools/)).
- **repeatability**: how closely the arm returns to a taught pose. PAROL6 specifies 0.1 mm ([Specifications](https://docs.source-robotics.com/PAROL-docs/1.1/03-technical-data/specifications/)).
- **RBTscript**: the program language of the commander software ([RBTscript](https://docs.source-robotics.com/PAROL-docs/1.1/programming/03-commander/rbtscript/)).

